174: ## RQ3: What do students report as their break-taking patterns (i.e., frequency and duration)?
175: 
176: As part of our investigation, we also inquired about how often students took breaks for different academic activities first on a broad scale from *never* (1) to *always* (5), where a rating of 3 was about half of the time. Students’ responses were intuitive. On average, student more consistently took a break while working on a large class project (*M* = 3.42, *SD* = 1.51), followed by studying for a quiz/exam (*M* = 3.09, *SD* = 1.06) and reading for class (*M* = 3.09, *SD* = 1.20), and lastly, they stated sometimes taking a break working on small class assignments (*M* = 2.44, *SD* = 1.14). They were also asked to estimate how many minutes their breaks typically lasted for each of these types of academic endeavors. The mean break time reported while working on a large class project was 22.99 minutes (*SD* = 19.18). The average break length while studying for a quiz or exam was 16.67 minutes (*SD* = 13.21), and 14.57 minutes when reading for class (*SD* = 14.19). Shorter breaks were recorded for working on a small class assignment (*M* = 8.86, *SD* = 11.87).
177: 
178: ## RQ3a: Does tendency to multitask predict break taking patterns (i.e., frequency and duration)?
179: 

178: ## RQ3a: Does tendency to multitask predict break taking patterns (i.e., frequency and duration)?
179: 
180: The associations between tendency to multitask and the frequency/duration of break variables were explored for all 4 types of academic activities via Pearson’s correlations. First exploring frequency, there was a significant association between multitasking tendency and frequency of breaks when studying for a quiz or exam (*r*(113) = .20, *p* = .036), but not for the other academic activities of working on a large class project (*r*(113) = .12, *p* = .196), reading for class (*r*(113) = .13, *p* = .180), nor working on a small class assignment (*r*(113) =. 17, *p* = .074). Similarly, Pearson’s correlations were used to discover whether there was an association between tendency to multitask and duration of breaks for these academic activities, specifically when working on a large class project (*r*(113) = .14, *p* = .134), studying for a quiz or exam (*r*(113) = .17, *p* = .073), reading for class (*r*(113) = .14, *p* = .137), and working on a small class assignment (*r*(112) =. 05, *p* = .632). As evidenced above, statistically significant correlations were not observed between tendency to multitask and break duration.
181: 
182: ## RQ3b: Does conscientiousness predict break taking patterns (i.e., frequency and duration)?
183: 

184: We also examined whether conscientiousness was related to break taking frequency or duration. A significant negative association existed between conscientiousness and frequency of breaks when working on a large class project (*r*(114) = -.27, *p* = .003), studying for a quiz or exam (*r*(114) = -.23, *p* = .012), and working on a small class assignment (*r*(114) = -.23, *p* = .015), but not for reading for class, *r*(114) = -.09, *p* = .347. Similarly, Pearson’s correlations were used to discover whether there was an association between conscientiousness and duration of breaks for these academic activities. Statistically significant negative correlations were observed between conscientiousness and break duration for each of these activities: when working on a large class project (*r*(114) = -.42, *p* < .001), studying for a quiz or exam (*r*(114) = -.21, *p* = .024), and working on a small class assignment (*r*(113) = -.30, *p* =
185: 
186: .001). However, as with frequency, there was not a significant correlation between conscientiousness and reading for class (*r*(114) = .11, *p* = .229).
