303: ## Discussion
304: 
305: The aim of the current study was to learn more about college students’ break-taking habits within the context of their study behaviors, individual differences in their break-taking patterns, and their perceptions of the value of break-taking while studying. We found that students have a variety of study behaviors, including breaks in their study habits (RQ1), engage in a variety of activities during their breaks (RQ2), and vary the frequency and duration of breaks based on the study task they are completing (RQ3). Tendency to multitask was not a strong predictor of break patterns (RQ3a) but conscientiousness did negatively predict both frequency and duration of breaks from studying for quizzes and exams, working on large class projects, and working on small class assignments (RQ3b). Students consistently recognized a variety of benefits of break-taking through qualitative and quantitative assessments. Students also reported disagreement with drawbacks tied to academic performance in quantitative items but spoke to drawbacks in their process of break-taking and studying in qualitative reports (RQ4). A relative importance analysis further clarified students’ perceptions of the benefits and drawbacks by relating them to the experience of rejuvenation. Several advantages of taking breaks positively predicted students’ perceptions of rejuvenation while several drawbacks of taking breaks negatively predicted students’ perceptions of rejuvenation (RQ5). The results from the current study suggest students do recognize the benefits of breaks and understand the potential effects of break-taking on their study process, consistent with a study of student perceptions of breaks in the classroom (Ferrer & Laughlin, 2017). Moreover, students’ quantitative reports that breaks aid in recovery and improve motivation while studying is consistent with experimental research on the effectiveness of physical activity breaks during lecture (Blasche et al., 2018; Fenesi et al., 2018; Young-Jones et al., 2022) and research supporting the cognitive
306: 
307: <!-- page 17 -->
308: 

309: Hamilton and Stachowski
310: 
311: resource theory explanation for the effectiveness of breaks (e.g., Ginn et al, 2023; Ross et al., 2014). Surprisingly, students’ responses to the quantitative items related to potential drawbacks of breaks yielded different results than the responses to the qualitative item. Students disagreed with the statements about potential negative *outcomes* of breaks, such as difficulty meeting school obligations or not meeting deadlines. In contrast, students did report on a number of drawbacks in their qualitative reflections. Notably, the vast majority of students included comments aligned with the theme of loss of concentration/focus (loss of motivation). Additionally, the majority of themes identified from the qualitative item reflected effects on their process of completing academic tasks, suggesting recognition of potential negative consequences for their *process* of studying. The results related to benefits and drawbacks is further confirmed by the relative weights analysis. Students’ perceptions of rejuvenation were most explained by the positive *outcome* of breaks helping them to be a better student and the potential negative *process* factor of students feeling that breaks take time they would rather be spending on schoolwork. Pham and Taylor (1999) concluded that study behavior and academic performance varies among students who engage in process- and outcome-based mental visualization. Combining our results with those of Pham and Taylor, it is possible that whether students focus on the process of studying versus the outcome of studying may affect their academic behavior and decisions. It also appears students do regulate the duration and frequency of break-taking behavior based on the type of academic task they are working on. Even so, students do not recognize breaks as part of their overall study strategy, as less than half of respondents included breaks in their description of their study approach. Indeed, taking breaks necessarily means students are using spaced learning approaches rather than a single study session, despite reporting taking breaks from a variety of different learning tasks. Previous research has shown the majority of students endorsed cramming as their study strategy (e.g., Hartwig & Dunlosky, 2012; Morehead et al., 2016). Thus, it is possible students do not connect break-taking with their academic preparation and performance. Interestingly, one of the most common break activities was checking social media, similar to a study of medical students’ study habits, in which the most common break behavior was using the internet (Alzahrani et al., 2018). The current findings are consistent with the research on media multitasking. For example, Rosen et al. (2013) noted that in a 15-minute study session, participants spent only about 10 minutes on task and the remaining time engaging in off-task behaviors, including social media use. In addition, others have found that engaging in media multitasking in the classroom affected academic performance (Bellur et al., 2015; Demirbilek & Talan, 2018). Researchers have proposed using technology breaks to reduce media multitasking (e.g., Lawson & Henderson, 2015; Rosen et al., 2013), but there appears to be a paucity of research on the effectiveness of social media as the break activity from academic tasks. However, evidence that cell phone use as a break activity may not improve task completion exists. Kang and Kurtzberg’s (2019) research revealed that students who used their cell phone to shop during a rest break from solving anagrams were less accurate in solving the anagrams than students who shopped via computer or paper catalog. It is important to note that research supports the effectiveness of a variety of different types of breaks, such as relaxation, physical activity, and listening to music (e.g., Blasche et al., 2017; Rees et al., 2017; Steinborn & Huestegge, 2016; for a review, see Albulescu, 2022). Our results are consistent with the broader literature on study habits, which reports students do not use effective study strategies (Hartwig & Dunlosky, 2012; Kornell & Bjork, 2007; Morehead et al., 2016). Another point of interest is the association (or lack thereof) of personality variables and break-taking habits. More conscientious students had a lower frequency of breaks and took shorter breaks than students who were less conscientious. These findings are consistent with previous research on academic performance and conscientiousness (e.g., Digman, 1989). Contrary to prediction, tendency to multitask was not associated with break patterns except for frequency of
