SOURCE should i study or take a break? students’ use and perceptions of study breaks - (hamilton & stachowski, 2025).md
<!-- page 1 --> L30: Much is known about effective study strategies, including use of spaced learning (for a review, see Cepeda et al., 2006) and active learning strategies like repeated testing (for a review, see Roediger & Karpicke, 2006). More recently, Hartwig and Malain (2022) studied students’ exam preparation behaviors during the course of an academic semester in real-world study of student study patterns. They observed the frequency and duration of study behaviors and the type of study behaviors (reading an online textbook and completing online practice quizzes) for an entire course. They discovered students who engaged in spaced completion of practice quizzes performed academically better than those students who studied closer to completing unit exams and/or those who read the textbook. However, research on study habits among college students demonstrates a difference between what students *should* do and what they *actually* do. Indeed, several studies have indicated that students’ study strategies are not particularly effective (e.g., Hartwig & Dunlosky, 2012; Karpicke et al., 2009; McCabe, 2011). When considering study behaviors, less well-known is the use of short rest periods or breaks as part of students’ study approach. Thus, it may be similarly useful to understand students’ typical break-taking behaviors within the context of their study habits. The underlying theoretical basis for the current study is cognitive resource theory (reviewed in Flanagan & Nathan-Roberts, 2019). Cognitive resource theory argues that completing a cognitively demanding task requires resources and those resources deplete over time (for a review, see Warm et al., 2008). Cognitive resource theory has been studied extensively in research on vigilance (e.g., Helton & Russell, 2012; Helton & Warm, 2008; Ross et al., 2014). Particularly relevant
<!-- page 2 --> L36: to the current study, taking breaks improved performance on vigilance tasks (Ross et al., 2014). The theory has been applied to the classroom as well. Ginn et al. (2023) asked participants to complete a series of timed arithmetic problems, followed by a quiet break, a break viewing a video of nature, or no break. Then the participants were asked to learn about strategies to do mental arithmetic and complete a test to assess their learning. Participants in the break conditions performed better on the post-lesson test than in the no-break condition, regardless of the type of break. Consistent with research on the cognitive resource theory, research on rest breaks has shown taking breaks can lead to improved performance on a variety of cognitive tasks (e.g., Bergum & Lehr, 1962; Lim & Kwok, 2016; for a review, see Albulescu et al., 2022). In other applied cognitive tests, the results are similar. For example, Steinborn and Huestegge (2016) concluded that taking a break improved reaction time in detecting errors in arithmetic problems compared to no break, particularly when the error detection task was more challenging and as the task progressed. Similarly, in a word editing task, participants were more accurate in their editing in the second half of the task if they received just a 40-second break than if they did not receive a break (Conlin et al., 2021). Participants who took a break between sets of anagram tasks solved more anagrams in the second set than those who were required to complete the second set without a break (Kang & Kurtzberg,
<!-- page 2 --> L38: 2019). It is also important to point out that the type of break may also matter, as Santos et al. (2020) determined participants who took an internet break did not improve their performance of either an attention task or a paper-folding task following that break. Many studies have evaluated the effectiveness of breaks on completion of academic performance and attention in the K-12 classroom (for a review, see Infantes-Paniagua et al., 2021). In contrast, research on breaks in the college classroom is somewhat limited and focused primarily on physical activity breaks. For instance, Blasche et al. (2018) compared self-selected break activities, physical exercise breaks, and relaxation breaks to no break on college students’ perceived vigor and fatigue during a four-hour lecture. They discovered college students felt invigorated and experienced less fatigue after a break of any type, but more so by taking an exercise break, both immediately subsequent to the break and 20 minutes later, compared to not taking a break at all. Fenesi et al. (2018) found three 5-minute physical breaks during a 50-minute lecture improved students’ perceptions of being on-task and performance on tests of their learning, compared to playing a computer game during the same breaks or not taking a break. Young-Jones et al. (2022) reported students experienced higher levels of motivation to continue a lecture after a break involving either yoga or a moderate level of physical activity, compared to a sedentary break, but did not score any better on a test of their learning from the lecture. Despite the limited research, a consistent finding is that breaks improve students’ level of energy and motivation during lecture. Knowing the discrepancy between what students *should* do and what they *actually* do in terms of study behaviors, we might also expect variability in how they approach taking breaks, despite the research supporting break-taking improving cognitive and academic performance. Research indicates that in the work environment, individual differences in break-taking choices exist. In one such study, Blasche et al. (2017) found those employees with greater intentions to take breaks were more likely to take breaks during their work shift. Additionally, the research revealed stronger break intentions were associated with lower levels of fatigue. Phan and Beck (2023) developed a compr
<!-- page 3 --> L56: A total of 116 undergraduate students completed a series of online questionnaires. The majority indicated being women/females (66%), with 29% identifying as men/male, 3% non-binary, and 3% preferring not to report their gender. Respondents could select multiple racial and ethnic identities, and as such, the numbers documented here exceed the total sample size. The majority of respondents reported being White (*n* = 104). Students also identified as Hmong (*n* = 3), African American or Black (*n* = 2), American Indian or Alaskan Native (*n* = 2), Native Hawaiian/Pacific Islander (*n* = 1), Vietnamese (*n* = 1), and Other Asian (*n* = 6). A total of 4 students recorded being of Hispanic or Latino/a origin: 2 of Mexican American or Chicano/a origin, and 2 of other Hispanic or Latino/a origin. The breakdown of tenure at the university was roughly even: 22% First-year students (1-29.5 credits, 22% Sophomores (30-59.5 credits), 31% Juniors (60-89.5 credits), and 25% Seniors (90+ credits).
<!-- page 5 --> L97: A total of 118 students reviewed the consent form and opened the survey. After data cleaning during which outliers were removed, along with incomplete responses, a total of 116 responses remained for further analyses. As needed, items were recoded, and alphas computed to examine the reliability of scales of items (i.e., break benefits, break drawbacks, and conscientiousness measures). Mean scores were then computed for these variables. One set of questions (*n* = 4) asking students to report their typical number of breaks while working on a variety of academic tasks was clearly misunderstood and so not included in the analyses presented below. Means, standard deviations, correlations, and reliabilities are presented in Table 1. Qualitative coding was completed by the authors of the paper during which initial codes were generated jointly. The authors independently coded all student responses and discrepancies were then resolved by consensus.
