<!-- page 10 --> L176: As part of our investigation, we also inquired about how often students took breaks for different academic activities first on a broad scale from *never* (1) to *always* (5), where a rating of 3 was about half of the time. Students’ responses were intuitive. On average, student more consistently took a break while working on a large class project (*M* = 3.42, *SD* = 1.51), followed by studying for a quiz/exam (*M* = 3.09, *SD* = 1.06) and reading for class (*M* = 3.09, *SD* = 1.20), and lastly, they stated sometimes taking a break working on small class assignments (*M* = 2.44, *SD* = 1.14). They were also asked to estimate how many minutes their breaks typically lasted for each of these types of academic endeavors. The mean break time reported while working on a large class project was 22.99 minutes (*SD* = 19.18). The average break length while studying for a quiz or exam was 16.67 minutes (*SD* = 13.21), and 14.57 minutes when reading for class (*SD* = 14.19). Shorter breaks were recorded for working on a small class assignment (*M* = 8.86, *SD* = 11.87).
<!-- page 10 --> L180: The associations between tendency to multitask and the frequency/duration of break variables were explored for all 4 types of academic activities via Pearson’s correlations. First exploring frequency, there was a significant association between multitasking tendency and frequency of breaks when studying for a quiz or exam (*r*(113) = .20, *p* = .036), but not for the other academic activities of working on a large class project (*r*(113) = .12, *p* = .196), reading for class (*r*(113) = .13, *p* = .180), nor working on a small class assignment (*r*(113) =. 17, *p* = .074). Similarly, Pearson’s correlations were used to discover whether there was an association between tendency to multitask and duration of breaks for these academic activities, specifically when working on a large class project (*r*(113) = .14, *p* = .134), studying for a quiz or exam (*r*(113) = .17, *p* = .073), reading for class (*r*(113) = .14, *p* = .137), and working on a small class assignment (*r*(112) =. 05, *p* = .632). As evidenced above, statistically significant correlations were not observed between tendency to multitask and break duration.
